<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Misiones Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Misiones is a province of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, a narrow territory between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> whose name recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions founded among the Guaraní in the 17th century, such as San Ignacio Miní. After the Jesuits were expelled in 1767 the region was disputed, and in the 1810s it joined the Federal League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a>. Its capital is Posadas, and it contains the Iguazú Falls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/misiones-province.jpg" alt="Misiones Province" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leandro Kibisz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Misiones_-_Parque_provincial_Salto_Encantado_desde_ruta_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
